B.e4J=new A.e(B.L,["The idea"],t.w)
B.e4f=new A.e(B.L,["What each layer does"],t.w)
B.e1c=new A.e(B.L,["How to read the chart"],t.w)
B.e6t=new A.e(B.L,["When it tends to work"],t.w)
B.e3o=new A.e(B.L,["When it tends to fail"],t.w)
B.e1J=new A.e(B.L,["Data it needs"],t.w)
B.e2t=new A.e(B.L,["Market data: {list}. Live through your forge when it is on; demo data otherwise."],t.w)
B.e2R=new A.e(B.L,["Market data: the chart symbol only. Live through your forge when it is on; demo data otherwise."],t.w)
B.e6o=new A.e(B.L,["Economic series (FRED): {list}. No source is set up yet, so the preview draws them from synthetic data."],t.w)
B.e0t=new A.e(B.L,["{n} votes read their own market or economic data inside TradingView. They stay neutral in this preview."],t.w)
B.e18=new A.e(B.L,["1 vote reads its own market or economic data inside TradingView. It stays neutral in this preview."],t.w)
B.e4F=new A.e(B.L,["Educational, not advice. A strategy describes how indicators behaved in the past; it does not predict prices. Test anything on paper before risking money."],t.w)
B.e5x=new A.e(B.L,["Multi-factor composite"],t.w)
B.e2b=new A.e(B.L,["Trend & momentum"],t.w)
B.e5b=new A.e(B.L,["Mean reversion & ranges"],t.w)
B.e6g=new A.e(B.L,["Macro regime & risk-off"],t.w)
B.e1u=new A.e(B.L,["Inflation & rates"],t.w)
B.e0F=new A.e(B.L,["Liquidity cycle"],t.w)
B.e1p=new A.e(B.L,["Credit stress & crisis hedge"],t.w)
B.e5J=new A.e(B.L,["Cross-asset ratios"],t.w)
B.e65=new A.e(B.L,["FX & commodities"],t.w)
B.e5d=new A.e(B.L,["Volatility"],t.w)
B.e5g=new A.e(B.L,["Trend Follower \xb7 Enhanced"],t.w)
B.e5D=new A.e(B.L,["15-vote trend and macro composite, gold and CPI context, a regression channel and recession shading."],t.w)
B.e4M=new A.e(B.L,["Prices that have been rising tend to keep rising for a while, and the economy decides how long. This blends six price-trend votes (time-series momentum counts double, EWMAC one and a half) with nine macro votes: fear (VIX), credit, money supply, the yield curve, high-yield spreads, real yields, unemployment, inflation and Fed liquidity. An ADX gate mutes the score when the market has no trend. It is your own Trend Follower script, rebuilt as layers."],t.w)
B.e3r=new A.e(B.L,["The composite: 15 votes from \u2212100 to +100. Tints candles, marks each flip with \u25b2\u25bc, shades past US recessions and drives the long/cash backtest."],t.w)
B.e2A=new A.e(B.L,["Gold drawn on the price chart. When gold leads and stocks lag, investors are usually buying protection."],t.w)
B.e6n=new A.e(B.L,["A one-year regression channel (\xb12 standard deviations) on price. Shows how stretched the trend is."],t.w)
B.e5t=new A.e(B.L,["CPI inflation, year on year, in its own panel. Rising inflation usually means tighter policy ahead."],t.w)
B.e2x=new A.e(B.L,["Above zero and green candles = the trend and the economy agree on risk-on. Below zero = step aside. A \u25b2 near the lower channel line is a cleaner entry than one at the top. Grey bands are past recessions: check how the score behaved going into them."],t.w)
B.e2Z=new A.e(B.L,["Long, persistent moves: 2003\u201307, 2009\u20132021 style bull runs and slow bear markets where the macro votes turn first."],t.w)
B.e5Y=new A.e(B.L,["Sharp V-shaped reversals (spring 2020) and sideways years, where the score flips back and forth and each flip costs a little."],t.w)
B.e3V=new A.e(B.L,["Nine of the votes are economic series that TradingView fetches when the script runs on your chart."],t.w)
B.e26=new A.e(B.L,["Dual Momentum: Stocks vs Bonds"],t.w)
B.e0o=new A.e(B.L,["Own the trend only when stocks are also beating bonds; volatility-sized."],t.w)
B.e1Y=new A.e(B.L,['Absolute momentum asks "is this going up?"; relative momentum asks "is it beating the alternative?". Requiring both keeps you out of rallies that bonds are winning, which often happen late in a cycle. Position size shrinks when volatility rises.'],t.w)
B.e3S=new A.e(B.L,["Absolute momentum: time-series momentum (double weight), EWMAC, a Donchian breakout and the 50/200 regime, gated by ADX and sized to a 15% volatility target. Drives the strategy backtest."],t.w)
B.e0H=new A.e(B.L,["SPY \xf7 TLT: rising = stocks beating long bonds (relative momentum)."],t.w)
B.e0C=new A.e(B.L,["One-year rolling correlation of price with the stocks\xf7bonds ratio. Near +1 means the chart moves with equity leadership."],t.w)
B.e44=new A.e(B.L,["Strongest when the composite is above zero AND the SPY\xf7TLT line is rising. If the composite is positive but the ratio is falling, bonds are winning: treat it as a weaker signal."],t.w)
B.e5z=new A.e(B.L,["Clear equity bull markets and long bond bear markets, like 2013\u20132019."],t.w)
B.e0J=new A.e(B.L,['Years when stocks and bonds fall together (2022), because both legs say "risk-off" and there is no winner to own.'],t.w)
B.e1I=new A.e(B.L,["SPY and TLT are market symbols; the ratio is one data request per leg."],t.w)
B.e6b=new A.e(B.L,["Regression Fade, Regime-Filtered"],t.w)
B.e55=new A.e(B.L,["Buy stretches to the lower band, only in the direction of the 200-day regime."],t.w)
B.e19=new A.e(B.L,["In a range, price tends to snap back toward its regression line. Fading every stretch is dangerous in a trend, so the RSI mean-reversion vote (double weight) is paired with the 50/200 regime: the mix only leans long when the bigger trend is up."],t.w)
B.e4E=new A.e(B.L,["RSI mean reversion (double weight) plus the 50/200 EMA regime. No ADX gate: this one wants the quiet, trendless stretches."],t.w)
B.e5C=new A.e(B.L,["One-year regression channel (\xb12 standard deviations): the slow fair-value line."],t.w)
B.e4h=new A.e(B.L,["One-quarter channel (\xb11.5): short-term stretch. A touch of its lower edge inside an up-sloping slow channel is the classic setup."],t.w)
B.e2P=new A.e(B.L,["VIX in its own panel. A VIX spike with price at the lower band often marks capitulation; a slowly rising VIX does not."],t.w)
B.e1b=new A.e(B.L,["Look for price at or under the fast channel's lower edge while the slow channel slopes up and the composite turns up (\u25b2). Exit ideas: the slow channel's middle line."],t.w)
B.e6A=new A.e(B.L,["Choppy, range-bound years such as 2015 or much of 2011, and pullbacks inside a steady uptrend."],t.w)
B.e4R=new A.e(B.L,['The start of a real bear market: the lower band keeps moving down and every "stretch" gets more stretched.'],t.w)
B.e1t=new A.e(B.L,["Runs on the chart symbol and VIX; everything else is computed from price."],t.w)
B.e5u=new A.e(B.L,["Credit-Gated Trend"],t.w)
B.e3K=new A.e(B.L,["Trend votes that defer to the bond market: junk-bond stress switches you off early."],t.w)
B.e1j=new A.e(B.L,["Credit markets are dominated by professional investors who price default risk first. High-yield bonds have often weakened before stocks top. Adding credit votes to a trend model aims to step aside earlier in a slow deterioration."],t.w)
B.e6k=new A.e(B.L,["Trend votes (TSMOM, EWMAC, Donchian) plus credit votes: the HYG/LQD ratio and the high-yield spread (each 1.5\xd7) and VIX. ADX gate, volatility-target sizing, strategy backtest."],t.w)
B.e15=new A.e(B.L,["HYG \xf7 LQD: junk bonds versus investment-grade bonds. Falling = investors demanding more for risk."],t.w)
B.e2y=new A.e(B.L,["One-year correlation of price with the credit ratio. When it turns strongly positive, stocks are trading on credit fears."],t.w)
B.e4P=new A.e(B.L,["Watch for the HYG\xf7LQD line rolling over while price still makes highs: a divergence. The composite will usually weaken before it flips; the \u25bc marks when the mix gives up."],t.w)
B.e5f=new A.e(B.L,["Credit-led downturns such as 2007\u201308 and the 2015\u201316 energy credit scare."],t.w)
B.e53=new A.e(B.L,["Shocks that start outside credit (a pandemic, a sudden policy move), where credit and stocks fall on the same day."],t.w)
B.e25=new A.e(B.L,["HYG and LQD are market symbols. The high-yield spread vote reads FRED data inside TradingView."],t.w)
B.e0O=new A.e(B.L,["Yield-Curve Recession Radar"],t.w)
B.e35=new A.e(B.L,["Curve inversion, re-steepening and the Sahm rule voting on recession risk."],t.w)
B.e13=new A.e(B.L,["When short-term yields rise above long-term yields (an inverted curve), US recessions have tended to follow, often a year or more later. The danger zone is usually the re-steepening, when the Fed starts cutting. The Sahm rule and jobless claims confirm the labour market is turning."],t.w)
B.e46=new A.e(B.L,["Curve votes (inversion, curvature, term premium, re-steepening 1.5\xd7) plus labour votes (Sahm rule 2\xd7, claims) and the 50/200 regime. Shades past recessions and marks flips."],t.w)
B.e0X=new A.e(B.L,["The 10y\u20132y and 10y\u20133m spreads. Below zero = inverted."],t.w)
B.e0y=new A.e(B.L,["A two-year regression channel on price: how far the market is from its trend as risk builds."],t.w)
B.e4I=new A.e(B.L,["Spreads crossing back above zero after a long inversion, with the composite turning down, is the classic late-cycle warning. Compare with the grey recession bands to see the lead times."],t.w)
B.e6r=new A.e(B.L,["Slow-burn cycles: before 2001 and 2008 the curve inverted well ahead of the downturn."],t.w)
B.e4G=new A.e(B.L,["Lead times vary from months to two years, so it is early more often than late. The 2022\u201324 inversion was not followed by an official recession."],t.w)
B.e1f=new A.e(B.L,["Mostly economic series. In the preview they are synthetic; the exported script reads the real ones in TradingView."],t.w)
B.e6c=new A.e(B.L,["Inflation & Real-Yield Hedge"],t.w)
B.e4W=new A.e(B.L,["Real yields, breakevens and inflation votes, read against gold."],t.w)
B.e37=new A.e(B.L,['Gold pays no interest, so it competes with inflation-protected bonds. When real yields fall, holding gold costs less and it has tended to rise. Inflation votes and the dollar add the "why".'],t.w)
B.e54=new A.e(B.L,["Real yield (2\xd7), breakeven inflation, CPI, core PCE, oil and the dollar. Positive = an environment that has favoured real assets."],t.w)
B.e4T=new A.e(B.L,["Gold on the price chart, for direct comparison."],t.w)
B.e39=new A.e(B.L,["The 10-year TIPS real yield. Falling = tailwind for gold."],t.w)
B.e0U=new A.e(B.L,["One-year beta of price to gold: above 1 = the chart moves more than gold does."],t.w)
B.e1M=new A.e(B.L,["Open it on GLD or a miner ETF. The composite rising while the real-yield panel falls is the textbook alignment. A rising real yield with gold still climbing is a divergence worth noticing."],t.w)
B.e2T=new A.e(B.L,["Falling-real-yield regimes such as 2001\u20132011 and 2019\u20132020."],t.w)
B.e4y=new A.e(B.L,["When central-bank or safe-haven buying drives gold regardless of yields; the link loosened noticeably after 2022."],t.w)
B.e30=new A.e(B.L,["Gold is a market symbol; real yields and inflation are FRED series."],t.w)
B.e2w=new A.e(B.L,["Liquidity Tide"],t.w)
B.e1a=new A.e(B.L,["Fed balance sheet, net liquidity and M2 impulse, read against growth stocks."],t.w)
B.e1i=new A.e(B.L,['"Don\'t fight the Fed." When the central bank adds money to the system (bond buying, a falling Treasury account or reverse repo), risk assets have tended to rise, with growth stocks most sensitive. When liquidity drains, the tide goes out.'],t.w)
B.e5k=new A.e(B.L,["Net liquidity (2\xd7), its impulse, M2 level and impulse, bank credit and the 50/200 regime."],t.w)
B.e33=new A.e(B.L,["Fed total assets and M2 money supply."],t.w)
B.e49=new A.e(B.L,["The Nasdaq-100 (QQQ) on the price chart: the most liquidity-sensitive large index."],t.w)
B.e4u=new A.e(B.L,["A one-year regression channel on price."],t.w)
B.e1T=new A.e(B.L,["Turning points in the impulse votes tend to lead. A composite that rolls over while QQQ is still at the top of its channel is the early warning."],t.w)
B.e6f=new A.e(B.L,["Quantitative-easing eras (2009\u20132014, 2020\u201321) and the 2022 tightening."],t.w)
B.e2N=new A.e(B.L,["When earnings or other forces dominate: in 2023 stocks rose while the Fed shrank its balance sheet."],t.w)
B.e0V=new A.e(B.L,["QQQ is a market symbol; the liquidity series are FRED data."],t.w)
B.e6_=new A.e(B.L,["Crisis Hedge: Credit Stress"],t.w)
B.e4e=new A.e(B.L,["High-yield spreads, financial conditions and VIX, with Treasuries as the hedge."],t.w)
B.e2d=new A.e(B.L,["In a crisis, investors sell risky debt and buy government bonds (flight to quality). This stack measures stress directly (spreads, the Chicago Fed's financial conditions index, VIX) and shows whether long Treasuries are doing their hedging job."],t.w)
B.e4s=new A.e(B.L,["High-yield spread (2\xd7), the credit curve, financial conditions, HYG/LQD and VIX. Negative = stress building. Recession bands shown."],t.w)
B.e5X=new A.e(B.L,["The ICE BofA high-yield option-adjusted spread. Spikes = credit panic."],t.w)
B.e1z=new A.e(B.L,["Long Treasuries (TLT) on the price chart: the classic crisis hedge."],t.w)
B.e43=new A.e(B.L,["Half-year correlation of price with TLT. Negative = bonds are hedging; positive = both falling together (no hedge)."],t.w)
B.e0T=new A.e(B.L,["Act on the combination: composite below zero, spread panel rising, and the stock\u2013bond correlation still negative means Treasuries should cushion the fall. A positive correlation is the 2022 warning: the hedge isn't working."],t.w)
B.e6p=new A.e(B.L,["Credit crises and deflationary shocks: 2008, early 2020."],t.w)
B.e29=new A.e(B.L,["Inflation shocks, where bonds and stocks fall together, and quick stress spikes that reverse within days."],t.w)
B.e0v=new A.e(B.L,["TLT is a market symbol; spreads and conditions are FRED data."],t.w)
B.e3P=new A.e(B.L,["Risk-On / Risk-Off Ratios"],t.w)
B.e68=new A.e(B.L,["Discretionary\xf7staples and copper\xf7gold ratio charts voting on risk appetite."],t.w)
B.e63=new A.e(B.L,['Ratio charts strip out the overall market and show preference. When consumers\' "wants" (discretionary) beat their "needs" (staples), and industrial copper beats safe-haven gold, investors are leaning into growth. Both ratios turning down is a defensive rotation.'],t.w)
B.e4c=new A.e(B.L,["Trend votes plus copper/gold, credit and dollar votes, ADX-gated."],t.w)
B.e1S=new A.e(B.L,["XLY \xf7 XLP: consumer discretionary versus staples."],t.w)
B.e32=new A.e(B.L,["Copper \xf7 gold: a growth versus fear barometer; it often tracks the 10-year yield."],t.w)
B.e2B=new A.e(B.L,["One-year correlation of price with XLY\xf7XLP."],t.w)
B.e3L=new A.e(B.L,["Both ratios rising with the composite above zero = broad risk-on. Price at highs while both ratios fall = narrow, defensive rally: be suspicious."],t.w)
B.e0Y=new A.e(B.L,["Early-cycle recoveries and late-cycle rotations into defensives."],t.w)
B.e0Z=new A.e(B.L,["Sector-specific news (one retailer, a mine strike) can move a ratio without saying anything about the economy."],t.w)
B.e2C=new A.e(B.L,["All market symbols: four data requests for the two ratios."],t.w)
B.e4H=new A.e(B.L,["Dollar & Commodity Cycle"],t.w)
B.e2X=new A.e(B.L,["A falling dollar has tended to lift commodities, emerging markets and foreign earnings."],t.w)
B.e5I=new A.e(B.L,["Most commodities are priced in dollars, so a weaker dollar makes them cheaper abroad and has tended to lift prices. The dollar trend (2\xd7), oil and copper/gold sit beside price-trend votes."],t.w)
B.e1D=new A.e(B.L,["Dollar (2\xd7, inverted), oil, copper/gold, TSMOM and EWMAC, ADX-gated."],t.w)
B.e3M=new A.e(B.L,["The US dollar index (DXY)."],t.w)
B.e2i=new A.e(B.L,["WTI crude on the price chart: the biggest commodity input to inflation."],t.w)
B.e1G=new A.e(B.L,["One-year correlation of price with the dollar. Strongly negative = the chart is a dollar trade."],t.w)
B.e38=new A.e(B.L,["Best on commodity, emerging-market or materials charts. A falling DXY panel with the composite above zero is the aligned case."],t.w)
B.e6F=new A.e(B.L,["Long dollar down-cycles such as 2002\u20132008."],t.w)
B.e20=new A.e(B.L,["Supply shocks (oil in 2022) where commodities and the dollar rise together."],t.w)
B.e0x=new A.e(B.L,["DXY and crude oil are market symbols."],t.w)
B.e40=new A.e(B.L,["VIX Regime Switch"],t.w)
B.e69=new A.e(B.L,["Trend exposure that shrinks as fear rises; VIX counts double."],t.w)
B.e2W=new A.e(B.L,["Calm markets tend to trend up slowly; fearful markets fall fast. Weighting the VIX vote double and sizing by volatility means the model holds less when the ride gets rough, which is when most drawdown happens."],t.w)
B.e0N=new A.e(B.L,["The 50/200 regime, TSMOM, VIX (2\xd7, inverted) and credit. ADX gate, volatility-target sizing, strategy backtest."],t.w)
B.e60=new A.e(B.L,["VIX in its own panel. Roughly: under 15 calm, 20\u201330 nervous, over 30 fearful."],t.w)
B.e4r=new A.e(B.L,["Half-year correlation of price with VIX. Usually negative; a rising-together market is unusual and worth noticing."],t.w)
B.e5i=new A.e(B.L,["Composite above zero and VIX falling = full risk. VIX jumping over its recent range flips the mix toward cash before the trend votes catch up."],t.w)
B.e2_=new A.e(B.L,["Volatility clusters: 2008, 2011, late 2018, 2020."],t.w)
B.e2l=new A.e(B.L,["Brief spikes that reverse within days (Feb 2018, Aug 2024): the switch sells the low and buys back higher."],t.w)
B.e5m=new A.e(B.L,["VIX is a market symbol (live via your forge)."],t.w)
